<p>A commonplace book is a personal collection of passages, observations, and half-thoughts. Mine is not beautiful. It is a mess of index cards, margin notes, and screenshots I will probably never revisit. I keep it anyway.</p>

<p>The point is not storage. Search engines can store things far better than I can. The point is the act of copying something by hand, which forces you to slow down enough to notice what you are taking.</p>

<h2>A conversation with yourself</h2>

<p>Over time, the book becomes a portrait of your attention. You can see which questions kept returning, which writers you trusted, which ideas you circled for years before they became your own.</p>

<p>I’ve started adding a small note to each entry: why I copied it. Not a summary, just a trace of the thought that was alive when I did. Those notes are often more useful than the quote.</p>

<p>A commonplace book is not a productivity system. It is a place to think in public with yourself. The value is in the keeping, not the having.</p>
